# Privacy & permissions

**Primary source:** Android's runtime permissions API
(`ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission`), the `POST_NOTIFICATIONS`
permission (Android 13+), and Play Console's **Data Safety** section —
all Google-native and, for Data Safety, Google-**mandated** for Play
Store submission. No third-party layer replaces any of these.

## Play Console Data Safety

- Every app submission declares, in Play Console, what data types are
  collected, whether they're shared with third parties, and why — this
  is Android's analog to iOS's `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` manifest, except
  declared through the Play Console UI/API rather than a file shipped in
  the binary.
- **Every third-party SDK added to the app** (Retrofit/OkHttp collects
  nothing on their own, but Sentry/PostHog/Firebase do) must be
  accounted for in the Data Safety form — check this as part of the ADR
  for adding any new third-party SDK (`android-adr.mdc` trigger #3), not
  after Play Console submission is rejected or flagged.
- Keep the Data Safety declaration in sync with actual behavior as
  features change — a stale declaration is a Play Store policy risk, not
  just a compliance nicety.

## Runtime permission requests

- Request a permission (location, camera, storage, contacts,
  microphone, notifications) **only at the moment the user takes an
  action that needs it** — never speculatively at launch or on a
  settings screen the user hasn't visited.
- Every permission has a clear in-app rationale shown before the system
  dialog when `shouldShowRequestPermissionRationale` is true — explain
  **why in product terms**, not a generic "this app needs access to your
  camera."
- After a permission is permanently denied ("don't ask again"), do not
  keep re-triggering the system dialog — offer a path to the app's
  system settings screen with context on why, if the feature is still
  relevant to the user's current action.
- Permission state is read through a `core` service wrapper, not queried
  ad hoc from multiple features, so there is one place that knows the
  current state and whether re-prompting is appropriate.

## Target SDK and permission behavior changes

- Android's permission model changes meaningfully release to release
  (scoped storage, `POST_NOTIFICATIONS`, one-time permissions,
  background location restrictions). Bumping `targetSdk`
  (`android-adr.mdc` trigger #5) is not a "just recompile" change — it
  can change the app's actual runtime permission behavior. Test the
  permission flows explicitly after any `targetSdk` bump, not just
  before Play Store's mandatory-target-SDK deadlines force it.

## Anti-patterns

- **Requesting location/camera/contacts access on first launch** before
  the user has done anything that needs it.
- **A generic or missing in-app rationale** before the system permission
  dialog.
- **Adding a third-party SDK without updating the Play Console Data
  Safety declaration** before shipping.
- **Ignoring `targetSdk` bump implications** on existing permission
  flows.

